msm8992-chi: bind talkman_batterydata to pmi8994_fg as well

msm8992-chi.dtsi hands qcom,battery-data to &pmi8994_charger only. The
fuel gauge is the consumer that actually needs the BV-T5E profile:
qpnp-fg's fg_batt_profile_init() is what loads qcom,fg-profile-data, and
without a profile the gauge falls through to "No available batterydata,
using OTP defaults" and reports an SOC that was never characterised for
this cell.

Today it still works by accident. In this kernel qpnp-fg looks the node
up with

    drivers/power/qpnp-fg.c:3679
    batt_node = of_find_node_by_name(node, "qcom,battery-data");

and of_find_node_by_name() is a global walk of of_allnodes starting
*after* `node`, not a search of the fg node's children or phandles. The
root-level talkman_batterydata node happens to sit after the fg node in
the flattened tree, so it is found. Anything that reorders root children
ahead of /soc silently drops the profile, and the failure mode is a wrong
battery percentage rather than a boot failure.

CAF binds it explicitly on both PMIC children, e.g.
arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8992-mtp.dtsi:791. qpnp-smbcharger does read
the property as a phandle (qpnp-smbcharger.c:2976), so keep that one.

Not included here, because there is no evidence for it on 4VM_08r:
msm8992-mtp.dtsi also sets qcom,ext-sense-type on the fg node, selecting
an external sense resistor instead of the PMI8994 internal one. Getting
that wrong scales POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CURRENT_NOW and the coulomb counter.
It needs an on-device check (charging current in
/sys/class/power_supply/bms/current_now against the charger's
qcom,fastchg-current-ma) before it is set either way.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mmo/msm8992-chi.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mmo/msm8992-chi.dtsi
@@ -145,6 +145,10 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&pmi8994_fg {
+	qcom,battery-data = <&talkman_batterydata>;
+};
+
 &pmi8994_charger {
 	qcom,battery-data = <&talkman_batterydata>;
 };
